WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.140
Every time you open a coding agent,

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.180
you're standing on hundreds of decisions someone

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.030
else made.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.980
Someone wrote the system prompt,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.080
chose the tools,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.360
and decided what survives when the conversation

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.400
gets compacted.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.700
When you use an agent,

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.430
those decisions are invisible.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.830
When you build one,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.110
every one of them is yours.

00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:23.536
Here's the uncomfortable truth.

00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:25.246
The core of an agent fits on

00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:26.356
a single page.

00:00:26.506 --> 00:00:27.746
Call the model,

00:00:27.746 --> 00:00:29.356
run the tools it asks for,

00:00:29.426 --> 00:00:30.666
feed the results back,

00:00:30.666 --> 00:00:31.556
and repeat.

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:34.196
That's why so many first attempts disappoint.

00:00:34.346 --> 00:00:36.006
The loop is the easy part.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:37.896
The work is everything around it.

00:00:38.568 --> 00:00:40.388
The first instinct is to make everything

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:41.228
an agent.

00:00:41.228 --> 00:00:42.148
Resist it.

00:00:42.148 --> 00:00:44.148
Most of the value comes from ordinary

00:00:44.148 --> 00:00:46.658
code with model calls at fixed points:

00:00:46.828 --> 00:00:48.088
fetch the ticket,

00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:49.188
classify it,

00:00:49.188 --> 00:00:50.218
draft a reply,

00:00:50.308 --> 00:00:51.418
queue it for a human.

00:00:51.448 --> 00:00:53.148
Save the open loop for work like

00:00:53.148 --> 00:00:53.588
debugging,

00:00:53.588 --> 00:00:55.938
where each step depends on the last.

00:00:56.248 --> 00:00:57.518
Deterministic skeleton,

00:00:57.648 --> 00:00:58.798
agentic muscles.

00:00:59.669 --> 00:01:00.929
The model has no memory.

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:01.729
Each turn,

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:03.329
it sees exactly what you send it

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.509
and nothing else.

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:05.229
So,

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:07.119
you build the prompt every turn.

00:01:07.169 --> 00:01:08.459
Stable instructions,

00:01:08.629 --> 00:01:09.839
the relevant state,

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:11.399
history compacted,

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:12.699
tool results trimmed.

00:01:12.709 --> 00:01:14.889
When a home -built agent gets confused,

00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:16.389
it's usually the context,

00:01:16.389 --> 00:01:17.319
not the model.

00:01:18.056 --> 00:01:19.566
Tools are the product.

00:01:19.776 --> 00:01:21.796
Sort every one by its side effects.

00:01:21.796 --> 00:01:23.446
Read tools run freely.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.486
Write tools stay inside the run.

00:01:25.776 --> 00:01:26.696
Irreversible ones,

00:01:26.696 --> 00:01:28.516
like sending an email or charging a

00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:29.056
card,

00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:30.016
go through an approval,

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:32.076
or aren't handed over at all.

00:01:32.076 --> 00:01:33.936
And make writes idempotent,

00:01:33.936 --> 00:01:35.276
because agents retry,

00:01:35.276 --> 00:01:37.926
and one refund should stay one refund.

00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:40.618
Asking a human is just another tool

00:01:40.618 --> 00:01:41.238
call.

00:01:41.238 --> 00:01:43.318
Picture a refund that needs a manager's

00:01:43.318 --> 00:01:44.818
sign -off and the manager is in

00:01:44.818 --> 00:01:45.358
a meeting.

00:01:45.358 --> 00:01:46.998
The harness saves the run,

00:01:46.998 --> 00:01:48.438
sends the question to Slack,

00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:49.368
and stops.

00:01:49.398 --> 00:01:50.828
18 hours later,

00:01:50.838 --> 00:01:51.998
the answer arrives,

00:01:51.998 --> 00:01:54.078
and the loop picks up exactly where

00:01:54.078 --> 00:01:54.948
it left off.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:57.351
So how do you know it works?

00:01:57.381 --> 00:01:59.031
You write evals:

00:01:59.161 --> 00:02:00.401
realistic tasks,

00:02:00.401 --> 00:02:01.591
each with a check.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:02.831
Start with twenty,

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.501
drawn from real traffic,

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:06.721
and turn every production failure into a

00:02:06.721 --> 00:02:07.571
new case.

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:09.761
Run them on every prompt tweak and

00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:11.131
every model upgrade.

00:02:11.181 --> 00:02:13.681
The builders who ship reliable agents aren't

00:02:13.681 --> 00:02:15.361
the ones with the cleverest prompts.

00:02:15.361 --> 00:02:17.391
They're the ones with the best evals.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:20.085
Treat every input as untrusted,

00:02:20.085 --> 00:02:21.585
and make less possible.

00:02:21.585 --> 00:02:22.955
Then comes the reward.

00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:24.565
Once you've built an agent,

00:02:24.565 --> 00:02:26.125
you get better at using one.

00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:28.365
The harness stops being a black box.

00:02:28.365 --> 00:02:29.285
Build one,

00:02:29.285 --> 00:02:30.535
even a small one.

00:02:31.005 --> 00:02:31.985
Next up:

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.415
when agents get it wrong.
